1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,300
So if you woke up tomorrow and suddenly found out you went completely viral overnight, I mean, how many hours do you think it would take to totally ruin your life?

2
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:14,260
Oh, wow. I mean, most people would probably say zero hours, right?

3
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:14,560
Right.

4
00:00:14,660 --> 00:00:17,300
Because society basically tells you it's a golden ticket.

5
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:28,780
Right. Exactly. Whether you are an entrepreneur trying to build a brand or a creative sharing your work online or, you know, just a really curious person navigating the Internet.

6
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:36,940
That society has drilled this one single directive into your head from day one, which is just go viral.

7
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:44,420
Yeah. We're essentially told that getting more eyes on our work is just like the ultimate goal, the guaranteed path to success.

8
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:52,720
But today we're actually looking at a mathematical proof that shows going viral is not a linear path to success at all.

9
00:00:52,720 --> 00:01:05,620
It's it's actually a highly volatile thermodynamic phase transition and quite possibly a structurally guaranteed path to your absolute disruption, which is quite the hook.

10
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:08,420
I know. Right. Welcome to the deep dive.

11
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:14,440
It really is the defining paradigm of contemporary digital culture, isn't it?

12
00:01:14,500 --> 00:01:14,660
Right.

13
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:24,820
We treat attention as this intrinsic good. We assume that if something is valuable, well, it should just be seen by as many people as humanly possible.

14
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:27,920
Yeah. Like the Internet is completely built on that premise.

15
00:01:27,920 --> 00:01:33,660
Exactly. It treats attention as a purely neutral resource like sunlight or water.

16
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:36,300
Yeah. You know, just absorb as much as you can and you'll grow.

17
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:46,160
OK, let's unpack this, because our mission for this deep dive today is to completely dismantle that modern cult of, you know, building an audience.

18
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:48,960
I love that phrasing, the cult of building an audience.

19
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:52,700
It really is one. And our source for today is dense.

20
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:57,920
It is mind bending. And it honestly completely shifted how I view the Internet.

21
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:11,840
It's a March 2026 paper by a researcher named Fliction, and it's titled Against Indiscriminate Visibility on the Structural Risks of Virality, Publicity and Exposure.

22
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:20,340
And what's so fascinating here is how Fliction challenges that fundamental illusion we just talked about, the illusion of neutral attention.

23
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:22,940
Right. Because attention isn't neutral, is it?

24
00:02:22,940 --> 00:02:30,400
Not at all. The paper rigorously proves that the digital attention economy is a highly unstable environment.

25
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:38,500
When you increase your visibility, you aren't just like casually expanding your opportunities or your customer base.

26
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:44,440
You are drastically expanding what the author calls your adversarial surface area.

27
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:51,360
Your adversarial surface area. I mean, just that phrase alone gives me a sense of impending doom.

28
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:52,940
It sounds militaristic, right?

29
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:58,680
It really does. It makes you sound like a military target, not a content creator or a small business owner.

30
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:04,220
But I want to start by challenging that core assumption of the Internet.

31
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:08,820
The idea that getting more eyes on your work is always a net positive.

32
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:15,900
Why is it that simply scaling up an audience actually degrades the quality of the feedback you get?

33
00:03:15,900 --> 00:03:22,500
Like, why can't we just have a million people giving us the exact same polite applause that 10 people would?

34
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,500
Right. The polite applause at scale.

35
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:25,040
Right.

36
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:33,920
To understand the mechanics of why that degradation happens, we have to look at the structural asymmetry between rumor and truth.

37
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,660
Okay. Structural asymmetry. Walk me through that.

38
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:45,060
So, in the physical world, or even in small, tight-knit digital communities, truth actually has a fighting chance.

39
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:48,000
And that's because verification is possible.

40
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:50,300
Because you know the people involved.

41
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:54,000
Exactly. You know them. Or you can go physically look at the thing being discussed.

42
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:06,740
But at scale, when you're dealing with millions of nodes, the propagation of rumor is mathematically much greater and faster than the propagation of truth.

43
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:08,960
Because truth is inherently slow.

44
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:10,740
Very slow. It is constrained.

45
00:04:10,980 --> 00:04:11,180
Yeah.

46
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:15,120
It requires evidence, requires context, and time to verify.

47
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:19,360
It's like truth has a speed limit enforced by reality.

48
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:23,420
Like, you have to actually check sources. You have to read the full context.

49
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:27,900
While a rumor is basically, I don't know, a sports car with no brakes.

50
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:29,380
That is a perfect way to put it.

51
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:33,360
Fast, cheap to produce, and it transmits effortlessly.

52
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:34,280
Yeah.

53
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:38,600
Because rumors are usually built on a highly emotional heuristic.

54
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:41,880
It doesn't ask you to think. It just asks you to react.

55
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:44,420
And that emotional hook is the accelerant.

56
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:45,400
Exactly.

57
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:52,400
And this creates a severe capacity bottleneck for anyone who achieves high visibility.

58
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:58,160
The correction of a falsehood does not scale proportionally with the falsehood itself.

59
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:01,320
Wait, say that again? The correction doesn't scale.

60
00:05:01,560 --> 00:05:11,280
Right. So if a single, unverified, damaging claim about you goes viral, it requires a massive, exhausting amount of effort to refute it.

61
00:05:11,280 --> 00:05:16,880
Oh, I see. You have to pull the records, write the statement, provide the context, do a whole video essay.

62
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:18,960
Yes. All that external work.

63
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:28,540
And even if you meticulously dismantle the lie, that refutation will literally never reach all the original recipients of the false claim.

64
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:33,540
Because the architecture of the network just doesn't support equal distribution of corrections.

65
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:37,220
The algorithm rewards engagement, not accuracy.

66
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:48,700
Exactly. The conditions that make the rumor spread, like the drama, the simple good versus evil narrative, the outrage, those are the exact conditions that make it almost impossible to dislodge.

67
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:54,000
Because truth is complicated and, let's be honest, often kind of boring.

68
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:06,660
Right. So when your visibility increases, your reputational attack surface grows exponentially faster than your physical or mental ability to defend it.

69
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:10,920
Okay, but this brings up a critical distinction in the paper that I found super interesting.

70
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:22,380
When we think of reputational attacks, we usually picture, like, coordinated trolls, bot networks, or malicious saboteurs.

71
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:23,760
The cartoon villains.

72
00:06:23,760 --> 00:06:27,220
Exactly. We picture villains plotting our downfall.

73
00:06:27,820 --> 00:06:34,060
But fliction emphasizes that this degradation doesn't require bad actors at all.

74
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:39,780
No, not at all. It is driven entirely by ordinary, everyday human motives.

75
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:42,940
Just scaled up to unnatural proportions.

76
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:47,180
And this is the part that honestly forced me to re-evaluate my own behavior online.

77
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:52,500
Just normal people doing normal things, but the sheer scale of it turns it toxic.

78
00:06:52,500 --> 00:07:00,340
Yeah. Think about community cohesion or self-protection or honestly just the casual transmission of a juicy piece of gossip.

79
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:00,840
Right.

80
00:07:01,040 --> 00:07:09,040
When a community is organized around a specific belief system or a specific market and you introduce a novel or disruptive idea to them,

81
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:12,180
they rarely see it as an intellectual invitation.

82
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:13,720
They perceive it as a threat.

83
00:07:13,720 --> 00:07:16,200
An existential provocation, yes.

84
00:07:16,760 --> 00:07:19,080
Their reaction is inherently defensive.

85
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:23,400
The paper uses a really grounded example here about a restaurateur.

86
00:07:23,400 --> 00:07:32,820
Let's say someone opens a new restaurant in a neighborhood and they manage to undercut local prices, offering a cheaper, more accessible product.

87
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:34,540
A classic market disruption.

88
00:07:34,800 --> 00:07:35,260
Exactly.

89
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:40,960
The incumbent business owners on that street experienced that as a tangible loss.

90
00:07:41,620 --> 00:07:48,360
The rational response for them isn't to walk over and congratulate the new chef on their supply chain efficiency.

91
00:07:48,660 --> 00:07:48,980
Right.

92
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:52,140
They aren't going to throw a party for the guy stealing their customers.

93
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:52,680
No.

94
00:07:53,540 --> 00:07:54,400
It's to resist.

95
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:58,500
So they might start a little rumor about the quality of the new guy's ingredients.

96
00:07:59,040 --> 00:08:01,860
Like, oh, he's definitely using frozen fish.

97
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:03,060
That's why it's so cheap.

98
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:08,200
And importantly, they aren't evil supervillains quarreling their mustaches when they do this.

99
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:08,560
Right.

100
00:08:08,680 --> 00:08:12,300
They are reacting to the topology of the evaluation system.

101
00:08:12,300 --> 00:08:21,200
In a system of relative evaluation, like competing for a limited customer base or even grading on a curve in the classroom,

102
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:26,340
one person's success inherently reduces another person's standing.

103
00:08:26,340 --> 00:08:33,300
Which means resentment in these systems is not a moral failure of the individuals involved.

104
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:37,180
It is a structural consequence of the environment.

105
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:41,220
So the conflict is baked into the geometry of the market itself.

106
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:49,600
Any increase in your visibility within a competitive landscape naturally induces adversarial flows.

107
00:08:49,800 --> 00:08:50,700
That's the core of it.

108
00:08:50,780 --> 00:08:51,100
Yes.

109
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:54,800
But, okay, I'm trying to find a loophole here, though.

110
00:08:55,340 --> 00:09:00,120
I hear what you're saying, but isn't pushback just part of putting yourself out there?

111
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:00,820
How do you mean?

112
00:09:01,140 --> 00:09:07,180
Like, if you want to be a thought leader or sell a product, shouldn't you just accept that some people won't like it?

113
00:09:07,540 --> 00:09:09,020
Let me try an analogy here.

114
00:09:09,420 --> 00:09:12,640
It's like opening a small artisanal bakery.

115
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:13,600
Okay.

116
00:09:13,700 --> 00:09:14,520
I like bakeries.

117
00:09:14,520 --> 00:09:14,840
Right.

118
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:20,580
So if you serve 10 locals who live on your street, you get nuanced, helpful feedback.

119
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:24,360
They might say, hey, a little more salt in the sourdough tomorrow, please.

120
00:09:24,580 --> 00:09:24,660
Sure.

121
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:26,640
Manageable feedback.

122
00:09:27,180 --> 00:09:27,700
Exactly.

123
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:46,860
But if a million people suddenly teleport into your bakery overnight, even if 99% of them are perfectly neutral or actively love the bread, the 1% who arbitrarily hate the smell of yeast or who are allergic to gluten and furious that you don't caver to them are going to try to burn the place down.

124
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:47,300
Yes.

125
00:09:47,740 --> 00:09:49,080
And the problem is...

126
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:53,160
You physically cannot bake bread and fight fires at the same time.

127
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:55,520
That captures the dynamic perfectly.

128
00:09:55,520 --> 00:09:59,080
The issue isn't that pushback exists.

129
00:09:59,860 --> 00:10:05,660
The issue is the ratio of incoming flow to your corrective capacity.

130
00:10:05,740 --> 00:10:06,580
The ratio.

131
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
The algorithm can put your bakery in front of a million people instantly.

132
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:13,460
Your distribution scales infinitely.

133
00:10:13,460 --> 00:10:25,940
But your personal bandwidth to defend your work, to clarify your intentions, and as you said, to sweep up the broken glass, that remains exactly the same as it was when you were serving 10 people.

134
00:10:26,020 --> 00:10:27,420
Because I'm just one person.

135
00:10:27,560 --> 00:10:28,020
Exactly.

136
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:28,320
Yeah.

137
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:32,060
You're a biological entity trying to deal with algorithmic flow.

138
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:32,560
Yeah.

139
00:10:32,680 --> 00:10:33,460
The fundamental mismatch.

140
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:42,320
And here's where it gets really interesting, because the paper doesn't just leave this as a cool sociological observation or a warning about burnout.

141
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:44,100
No, it takes it much further.

142
00:10:44,100 --> 00:10:53,320
It asks, if rumor scales faster than truth, and if human capacity is fixed, how do we systematically measure this risk?

143
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:57,620
And this is where affliction does something entirely unexpected.

144
00:10:58,100 --> 00:11:02,340
They model internet virality using theoretical physics.

145
00:11:02,600 --> 00:11:04,160
The RSVP framework.

146
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:04,560
Right.

147
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:06,760
The relativistic scalar vector plenum.

148
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:09,680
I mean, it sounds incredibly intimidating on the surface.

149
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:10,700
It sounds terrifying.

150
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:14,800
But the underlying logic is actually quite elegant.

151
00:11:15,180 --> 00:11:25,460
It takes all these informal observations we just discussed about internet pylons, cancel culture, and virality, and formalizes them into a field theoretic model.

152
00:11:25,680 --> 00:11:32,740
It maps social dynamics onto the physics of fluid dynamics and electromagnetism, which is wild.

153
00:11:32,740 --> 00:11:34,220
It is wild, but it works.

154
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:42,600
So I want to break down the three main variables of this RSVP framework, and I want to make sure we keep it grounded so we don't lose anyone in the math.

155
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:43,140
That's good.

156
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:43,740
Let's do it.

157
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:43,960
Okay.

158
00:11:44,060 --> 00:11:45,200
First, we have visibility.

159
00:11:45,840 --> 00:11:49,160
The paper uses the Greek letter phi for this.

160
00:11:49,660 --> 00:11:51,100
This is the scalar potential.

161
00:11:51,300 --> 00:11:58,020
In physics, a scalar field just assigns a single value to every point in space, like temperature in a room.

162
00:11:58,480 --> 00:11:58,660
Right.

163
00:11:59,020 --> 00:11:59,440
Exactly.

164
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:06,000
In this model, phi is simply the amount of exposure you have, how hot your spotlight is.

165
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:06,440
Okay.

166
00:12:06,580 --> 00:12:06,980
Got it.

167
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:08,480
Visibility is phi.

168
00:12:08,920 --> 00:12:14,900
Moving to the second variable, we have interpretive flow, which is denoted by a vector V.

169
00:12:15,100 --> 00:12:17,080
A vector, so it has direction.

170
00:12:17,420 --> 00:12:17,700
Right.

171
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:22,880
If the scalar potential is the temperature in the room, the vector field is the wind.

172
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:25,580
It has both magnitude and direction.

173
00:12:25,580 --> 00:12:33,260
This represents the actual movement of opinions, attention, opportunism, and hostility across the network.

174
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:37,560
It's the current of human reaction moving toward or away from your work.

175
00:12:37,560 --> 00:12:38,440
Precisely.

176
00:12:38,900 --> 00:12:42,100
And third, we have reputational entropy, or S.

177
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:45,100
And entropy in physics is a measure of disorder.

178
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:45,600
Right.

179
00:12:45,720 --> 00:12:54,020
So here, it is the measurement of distortion, the density of rumors, and the overall narrative chaos surrounding your name or your brand.

180
00:12:54,020 --> 00:13:00,260
Okay, so we have visibility, interpretive flow, and entropy, phi, V, and S.

181
00:13:00,260 --> 00:13:08,240
And when you combine these three variables into a set of differential equations, the math reveals something profound about the architecture of the internet.

182
00:13:08,940 --> 00:13:16,080
As visibility, your phi, increases, it inevitably steepens the gradients of the social field.

183
00:13:16,480 --> 00:13:22,260
Let me translate this math into a visual, because this totally clicked for me when I was reading your notes on this.

184
00:13:22,360 --> 00:13:22,920
Go for it.

185
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:28,460
Visibility isn't just a spotlight shining on you, passively illuminating your work.

186
00:13:28,960 --> 00:13:30,920
It's a gravitational well.

187
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:32,480
A gravitational well.

188
00:13:32,560 --> 00:13:37,180
Yes, the heavier you get, the more visible you become, the stronger your gravity.

189
00:13:37,820 --> 00:13:43,320
And the stronger your gravity, the more space debris you aggressively pull into your orbit.

190
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:45,420
That's a great way to visualize it.

191
00:13:45,420 --> 00:13:52,620
You pull in the interpretive flow, critics, opportunists, people having a bad day who just want to yell at someone on their lunch break.

192
00:13:52,760 --> 00:13:54,020
You pull them all in.

193
00:13:54,020 --> 00:14:01,520
Because you are increasing your coupling to a highly heterogeneous environment.

194
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:04,100
Heterogeneous meaning mixed.

195
00:14:04,360 --> 00:14:04,540
Right.

196
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:07,240
Meaning people have vastly different priors.

197
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:07,560
Yeah.

198
00:14:07,820 --> 00:14:10,660
Different political triggers, different personal agendas.

199
00:14:10,660 --> 00:14:22,160
When your massive gravity pulls in all those different interpretive frameworks at once, that massive influx inevitably and mathematically generates reputational entropy.

200
00:14:22,760 --> 00:14:24,620
So the distortion isn't an accident.

201
00:14:24,800 --> 00:14:25,420
Not at all.

202
00:14:25,700 --> 00:14:25,860
Yeah.

203
00:14:25,920 --> 00:14:32,100
It's a product of the friction between all those conflicting interpretations colliding around your work.

204
00:14:32,220 --> 00:14:32,980
But hold on.

205
00:14:33,180 --> 00:14:34,580
Let me clay devil's advocate here.

206
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:38,360
What if you're right?

207
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:40,080
Right about what?

208
00:14:40,080 --> 00:14:41,340
Whatever you post.

209
00:14:42,380 --> 00:14:48,620
What if your viral post is morally pure, factually accurate, and ethically aligned?

210
00:14:48,780 --> 00:14:52,180
What if you're just posting, I don't know, a fundraiser for a children's hospital?

211
00:14:53,080 --> 00:14:57,160
Doesn't the truth value if your post protect you from that space debris?

212
00:14:57,660 --> 00:15:01,200
Because we are raised to believe the truth is a shield.

213
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:02,500
Yeah, we are.

214
00:15:02,860 --> 00:15:06,660
But this is perhaps the most sobering conclusion in the entire paper.

215
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:15,820
Fliction explicitly proves that moral alignment or factual correctness does not protect you from reputational entropy.

216
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:16,680
Wait, really?

217
00:15:16,740 --> 00:15:18,040
It offers zero protection?

218
00:15:18,280 --> 00:15:18,640
None.

219
00:15:19,340 --> 00:15:23,540
The intrinsic truth value of a signal does not stabilize the equation.

220
00:15:24,400 --> 00:15:29,060
Within this mathematical framework, correctness is not a stabilizing term.

221
00:15:29,060 --> 00:15:33,880
Entropy is driven by interaction density, not by truth.

222
00:15:33,880 --> 00:15:34,800
Oh, wow.

223
00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:38,480
I really want you, the listener, to pause and consider that.

224
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:40,180
It does not matter how right you are.

225
00:15:40,240 --> 00:15:42,220
It doesn't matter how pure your intentions are.

226
00:15:42,420 --> 00:15:49,680
If a million people are interacting with your statement, the sheer density of that interaction guarantees that the meaning will distort.

227
00:15:49,680 --> 00:16:03,000
Like, people will argue about the administrative costs of the children's hospital you're raising money for, or they will accuse you of using it for a tax write-off, or they will claim you're ignoring a different, more important disease.

228
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:14,420
And the signal degrades faster than it can be transmitted, because rumor, cynicism, and distortion are what physicists would call low-energy excitations.

229
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:17,920
Low-energy excitations, meaning they're easy to do.

230
00:16:18,100 --> 00:16:20,420
Incredibly cheap and easy to produce.

231
00:16:21,100 --> 00:16:24,100
Someone can tweet a cynical accusation in four seconds.

232
00:16:24,100 --> 00:16:54,020
